  "text": "Assistant: To find the area of the circle inscribed in the square, we need to determine the side length of the square and then use that to find the radius of the inscribed circle.\n\nStep 1: Find the midpoints of the sides of the triangle.\nThe triangle has vertices \\(A(0,0)\\), \\(B(5,0)\\), and \\(C(2,7)\\).\n\n- Midpoint of \\(AB\\): \\(\\left(\\frac{0+5}{2}, \\frac{0+0}{2}\\right) = \\left(2.5, 0\\right)\\)\n- Midpoint of \\(BC\\): \\(\\left(\\frac{5+2}{2}, \\frac{0+7}{2}\\right) = \\left(3.5, 3.5\\right)\\)\n- Midpoint of \\(CA\\): \\(\\left(\\frac{2+0}{2}, \\frac{7+0}{2}\\right) = \\left(1, 3.5\\right)\\)\n\nStep 2: Find the side length of the square.\nThe side length of the square is the distance between any two adjacent midpoints. Let's use the distance between the midpoints of \\(AB\\) and \\(BC\\):\n\n\\[ \\text{side length} = \\sqrt{(3.5 - 2.5)^2 + (3.5 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{1^2 + 3.5^2} = \\sqrt{1 + 12.25} = \\sqrt{13.25} = \\sqrt{\\frac{53}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{53}}{2} \\]\n\nStep 3: Find the radius of the inscribed circle.\nThe radius of the inscribed circle in a square is half the side length of the square. So, the radius \\(r\\) is:\n\n\\[ r = \\frac{\\text{side length}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{53}}{2}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{53}}{4} \\]\n\nStep 4: Find the area of the circle.\nThe area \\(A\\) of the circle is given by the formula \\(A = \\pi r^2\\):\n\n\\[ A = \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{53}}{4}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{53}{16}\\right) = \\frac{53\\pi}{16} \\]\n\nSo, the area of the circle is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{53\\pi}{16}}\\).",
